														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4438366">
									

<p>I think the writing is on point in terms of clinical tone, and the bird concept has the potential to be an SCP. However, I don't necessarily think the proposed canon has potential, and if it does, I don't think it should be included as the second half of a long SCP article - maybe you could write a canon/tale and backlink it to your article?</p>
<p>Because the way it's written now, it's not just your article that should have these heavily revised procedures, but <em>every</em> article. Trying to introduce a full overhaul of the state of the Foundation, relationships with major governments and GoIs, as well as all containment procedures on all articles is technically possible, but I just don't see people going along with it.</p>
<p>This SCP is a pet bird that opens locks. In terms of upending our understanding of the Foundation and all containment procedures, there's not much to draw people in or convince them to accept this new canon. If you really do want to push the canon, maybe shorten it down. A lot of it seems like redundant writing, and from a narrative point of view, reading very extensive containment procedures that ended up being fairly irrelevant to the nature of the SCP itself was a bit of a let down. There might be a better way to get this idea across, like in a single, shorter addendum rather than the current format.</p>

							</div>

								
